Let me look at the `change_pin` function one more time, specifically the `newpin` handling:

```c
newpin = (u8 *) opt_newpin;
while (newpin == NULL) {
    u8 *newpin2;

    newpin = get_pin("Enter new PIN", pin_obj);
    if (pinpad_present && newpin == NULL)   {
        if (verbose)
            printf("New PIN value will be prompted with pinpad.\n");
        break;
    }
    if (newpin == NULL || strlen((char *) newpin) == 0)   {
        fprintf(stderr, "No new PIN value supplied.\n");
        free(newpin);
        if (opt_pin == NULL)
            free(pincode);
        return 2;
    }

    newpin2 = get_pin("Enter new PIN again", pin_obj);
    if (newpin2 && strlen((char *) newpin2) &&
                    strcmp((char *) newpin, (char *) newpin2) == 0) {
        free(newpin2);
        break;
    }
    printf("PIN codes do not match, try again.\n");
    free(newpin);
    free(newpin2);
    newpin=NULL;
}
```

If `opt_newpin` is NULL and `pinpad_present` is false, `get_pin` is called. If `get_pin` returns NULL (because `opt_use_pinpad` is set), we hit:
```c
if (newpin == NULL || strlen((char *) newpin) == 0)   {
    fprintf(stderr, "No new PIN value supplied.\n");
    free(newpin);  // free(NULL) - fine
    if (opt_pin == NULL)
        free(pincode);  // if opt_pin is NULL, free pincode
    return 2;
}
```

If `opt_pin` is NULL and `pincode` was set by `get_pin` (malloc'd), `free(pincode)` is fine. If `pincode` is NULL (because `get_pin` returned NULL), `free(NULL)` is fine.

But what if `opt_pin` is NULL, `pinpad_present` is true, and `get_pin` returns NULL? Then `pincode` is NULL. Then in the `newpin` loop, `get_pin` returns NULL, and `pinpad_present && newpin == NULL` is true, so we break with `newpin == NULL`.

Then:
```c
r = sc_pkcs15_change_pin(p15card, pin_obj,
                pincode, pincode ? strlen((char *) pincode) : 0,
                newpin, newpin ? strlen((char *) newpin) : 0);
```

`pincode` is NULL, `newpin` is NULL. So `sc_pkcs15_change_pin` is called with NULL pins.

In `sc_pkcs15_change_pin`:
```c
if((!oldpin || !newpin)
        && (p15card->card->reader->capabilities & SC_READER_CAP_PIN_PAD
                || p15card->card->caps & SC_CARD_CAP_PROTECTED_AUTHENTICATION_PATH)) {
        data.flags |= SC_PIN_CMD_USE_PINPAD;
        ...
}
```

If the reader has PIN pad capability, `SC_PIN_CMD_USE_PINPAD` is set. Then `sc_pin_cmd` is called. If the card driver doesn't support `pin_cmd`, it goes to the else branch and returns `SC_ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED`. Then `change_pin` returns 2. No crash.

But what if the card driver DOES support `pin_cmd`? Then `card->ops->pin_cmd(card, data, tries_left)` is called. This could be where the crash happens.

